        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>How many calories does a walking pad burn in an hour?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              By standard MET estimates, an hour of flat walking at 2 mph burns roughly 150 to 230 calories in total for adults between about 130 and 200 lb. The extra over sitting at a desk is roughly 75 to 110 calories. At 3 mph the extra rises to roughly 120 to 180. These are estimates, and individuals can differ from them.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you lose weight just by walking on a walking pad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Possibly, but slowly, and only if your eating does not rise to match. Two hours a day at 2 mph adds very roughly 900 extra calories a week for a 160 lb person. That is helpful over months, yet it is easily cancelled by a daily snack. Most people who lose weight successfully combine more movement with changes to diet.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How long should I walk on a walking pad each day?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Start with 20 to 30-minute blocks and build towards one to two hours spread across the day if your feet and legs are comfortable. Several shorter blocks suit most people better than one long stint. Wear supportive shoes, keep the speed at a level where you can still work, and increase the total gradually over weeks.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What speed should I walk on a walking pad to lose weight?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The fastest speed you can sustain while doing your work, which for most people is around 1.5 to 2.5 mph. Faster burns more per hour, but a speed you cannot keep up for long burns less in total. Adding a short brisk block away from the keyboard is a useful way to raise the daily figure.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a walking pad as good as a treadmill for weight loss?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Per hour, no. A treadmill allows incline and faster speeds, which cost far more energy than slow flat walking. In total, it depends on use. Someone who walks two hours a day at a desk may accumulate more activity than someone who rarely uses a treadmill. The better machine is the one you will use most.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Are walking pad calorie counts accurate?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Treat them as rough guesses. Many displays assume a body weight unless you enter yours, and most report gross calories, which include what you would have burned sitting still. Wrist trackers are estimates too. Use the figures to compare your own days with each other rather than as a precise count to eat against.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

